  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nUpstream lightning activity has decreased significantly and will\ncontinue the trend as it moves into the CWA later this morning.\nShowers that have crossed the CWA to the ene early this morning have \nproduced a trace of rainfall and are just prepping the atmosphere for\na round of showers and thunderstorms this morning. There will be \nbreaks in the showers at times, and the thunderstorms will be \nassociated with vorticity maxima that are not currently present to be\ntimed with any certainty. Tornadic threat is due to closely spaced\nlow pressure centers at the surface and a little more to the west\naloft. Models are in fair agreement with the overall forecast, and \nare definitely put us in a dry slot overnight, beginning later this \nevening. \n \nThe most concerning time for severe weather and flooding potential \nwill be during the afternoon as a low pressure system nears the \narea. There is decent instability and shear through a deep layer. \nLCLs are also expected to be low. While the threat is conditional, \nthere is the potential for a few short lived tornadoes to occur in \nthis environment. The greatest threat area for tornadoes will be \neastern Indiana, portions of northern Kentucky and southwest Ohio. \nAnother potential threat will be damaging winds, however DCAPE\nremains not overly impressive. \n\nThe greatest threat area for flooding will also be the same area as\ntornadoes, aligned with more significant storm structures. Storms\nwill be slow moving, and some few areas may be hit with training\nstorms that will significantly increase the threat of flooding.\n \nAdditional thunderstorm on Sunday will be a continuation from the\nsame slow moving low pressure systems with widespread showers and \nsome thunderstorms. The greater severe threat will be east of the \nScioto River Valley and in the Hocking Hills. Cannot rule out a \nstrong to severe storm. In addition, localized heavy rain and \nflooding will remain possible.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nOff and on thunderstorm chances will continue into the work week. The\npath of the upper low remains in a high state of uncertainty, though\na number of models show it crossing the CWA Monday and then\nretrograding back into the region on Tuesday. It doesn't exit until \nsometime Wednesday. There are indications of another system\npotentially affecting the CWA and region on Friday.",
